<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hyecho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">704–<a href="/years/787">787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hyecho was a Buddhist monk from Silla who studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and in the 720s travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, visiting the holy places of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and returning through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. His account, Memoir of a Pilgrimage to the Five Kingdoms of India, was lost until a fragment was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> in 1908. He spent the rest of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> translating Buddhist texts.</p></div>
